From: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: wire in flow offload, off by 
 default

Bind the flowtable block, set NETIF_F_HW_TC, and program the ports' L3
interfaces, but accept no flow until the module parameter flow_offload
is set (/sys/module/qcom_ppe/parameters/flow_offload, or "options
qcom_ppe flow_offload=1"). Until then the block is bound, so a ruleset
with "flags offload" loads, every flow is declined, and no offload
register is written.

Enabling, under RTNL and then the driver lock, checks that L3_ROUTE_CTRL
sends routing failures to the CPU, saves every register it is about to
change, programs the exceptions and each EDMA port's L3 interface,
MY_MAC and L3_VP_PORT entries, and reads everything back. A port entry
that does not read back restores the saved values and leaves offload
off; an exception group that does not read back leaves it on with the
flows that rely on the group declined (every flow for the header
exceptions, TCP flows for the TCP flag ones), as the enable line says.
Disabling declines from then on, deletes every flow entry (each delete
read back) and the entries they point at, and only then writes the saved
registers back, the bits another user owns (a port's MTU) as they are
then. A flow entry that cannot be deleted, on any path, may still
forward: what it relies on is kept, every new flow is declined, and
enabling is refused until a reboot; so it is when a saved register does
not read back as saved.

On top of the engine:
- a rule with a dissector key the driver does not interpret is declined;
- the header exceptions punt to the CPU, on a flow hit and without
  de-accelerating the flow, what the software fast path would not
  forward itself: unknown L2, IPv4 options, short IHL, truncated or
  inconsistent headers, a bad header checksum, fragments, an expiring
  TTL, unpullable TCP and UDP headers; IPv4 is declined unless all read
  back. A TCP SYN is redirected without de-accelerating too, as the
  software fast path forwards it on a live flow; FIN and RST still
  de-accelerate. The L3 interfaces' TTL-exceeded action no longer de-accelerates
  either: a remote peer must not be able to take a flow out of hardware
  with one packet;
- while offload is on, a frame too big for its egress port goes to the
  CPU instead of being dropped (MRU_MTU_CTRL's MTU command; the MTU bits
  stay the port's): what the PPE inserts itself can take a full-size
  frame past the port's MTU at zero margin, after the flow hit;
- a flow already in hardware that the flowtable re-offers is stale when
  its entry no longer reads VALID or has DE_ACCE set, and is programmed
  afresh; hit counters are not used. More than 256 entries found no
  longer VALID within 10 s turn offload off, unless it was disabled and
  enabled again meanwhile; de-accelerated and superseded entries are
  re-armed without counting, so nothing a peer sends can turn it off;
- a re-offer that is now declined takes the flow's entry out of
  hardware;
- deleting a flowtable unbinds its block callbacks before it destroys
  its flows, so each flow records the block it came through and the
  driver's last callback on a block takes that block's flows out of
  hardware when it is released;
- a flow's references are let go of only once its entry is confirmed
  deleted. The tracking entry is allocated before the flow entry is
  programmed, and ppe_flow_entry_add() now reports an add that failed but
  may have landed (-EUCLEAN: its counter clear failed and taking it back
  out did not read back invalid, or its result never came), so an entry
  that may be in hardware after a failed add or insert stays tracked for
  disable to retry; one at an index hardware never returned keeps disable
  from writing anything back. An entry that cannot be deleted is counted
  and logged once each time it becomes stuck, and a re-offer no longer
  retries it (DESTROY, unbind, disable and a re-point do). Unload deletes every tracked flow, read back, and
  leaves the tables they point at as they are while one stays;
- edma_port_l3_if_update() becomes edma_port_l3_if_program(), which
  returns an error, and is called on MAC address and MTU changes only
  while offload is on; NETIF_F_HW_TC and the .ndo_setup_tc hook (Kamil
  Bienkiewicz's 0420 and 0423) are set here;
- the engine's indirect block binding for bridges and VLAN devices is
  dropped: an nft flowtable reaches it with TC_SETUP_FT, which it never
  handled, every flowtable flow is offered to the ports' own callbacks
  anyway (fw4 resolves bridges and VLANs to their ports), and it kept a
  callback list shared with the direct path, which tc and nft modify
  under different locks; the direct callbacks are on no list;
- /sys/kernel/debug/ppe/offload_flows lists the driver's own state and
  counters without reading a register; offload_flows_hw reads each
  tracked flow's VALID and DE_ACCE, for the bench at idle.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
